### What is selection sort?

Selection sort divides the array into two parts: a sorted prefix on the left and an unsorted suffix on the right. On each pass, it scans the unsorted suffix to find the minimum element and swaps it into the first unsorted position, growing the sorted prefix by one.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class SelectionSort {

    public static void selectionSort(BigDecimal[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            int minIdx = i;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (arr[j].compareTo(arr[minIdx]) < 0) {
                    minIdx = j;
                }
            }
            if (minIdx != i) {
                BigDecimal temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[minIdx];
                arr[minIdx] = temp;
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] balances = {
            new BigDecimal("75000"),
            new BigDecimal("25000"),
            new BigDecimal("50000"),
            new BigDecimal("10000")
        };
        selectionSort(balances);
        System.out.println("Sorted: " + Arrays.toString(balances));
    }
}
```

```text
Sorted: [10000, 25000, 50000, 75000]
```

### How does each pass work?

The outer loop marks the boundary between sorted and unsorted. The inner loop scans the unsorted suffix, tracking the index of the smallest element. After the inner loop, one swap places that element at the boundary.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class SelectionSortTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {64, 25, 12, 22, 11};
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            int minIdx = i;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (arr[j] < arr[minIdx]) minIdx = j;
            }
            int temp = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[minIdx]; arr[minIdx] = temp;
            System.out.println("Pass " + (i+1) + ": min=" + arr[i]
                + " -> " + java.util.Arrays.toString(arr));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Pass 1: min=11 -> [11, 25, 12, 22, 64]
Pass 2: min=12 -> [11, 12, 25, 22, 64]
Pass 3: min=22 -> [11, 12, 22, 25, 64]
Pass 4: min=25 -> [11, 12, 22, 25, 64]
```

### Why is the swap count special?

Selection sort performs exactly one swap per pass, so at most n - 1 swaps total. This is the fewest swaps of any simple sort. When writes are expensive, such as on flash memory or when each element is a large object costly to copy, selection sort minimises the number of moves.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class SwapCount {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {50, 40, 30, 20, 10};
        int swaps = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
            int minIdx = i;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
                if (arr[j] < arr[minIdx]) minIdx = j;
            }
            if (minIdx != i) {
                int t = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[minIdx]; arr[minIdx] = t;
                swaps++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Total swaps: " + swaps + " (max n-1 = " + (arr.length-1) + ")");
    }
}
```

```text
Total swaps: 2 (max n-1 = 4)
```

### Why is selection sort not stable?

The basic version is unstable because the long-distance swap can jump an equal element past another. For example, if two equal values appear and the one later in the array gets swapped to the front, the original order of those equal elements is disrupted.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class SelectionUnstable {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Two elements with value 5: "5a" at index 0, "5b" at index 2
        int[] vals = {5, 3, 5, 1};
        String[] tags = {"5a", "3", "5b", "1"};

        // Selection sort
        for (int i = 0; i < vals.length - 1; i++) {
            int minIdx = i;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < vals.length; j++) {
                if (vals[j] < vals[minIdx]) minIdx = j;
            }
            int tv = vals[i]; vals[i] = vals[minIdx]; vals[minIdx] = tv;
            String ts = tags[i]; tags[i] = tags[minIdx]; tags[minIdx] = ts;
        }

        System.out.println("Result: " + java.util.Arrays.toString(tags));
        System.out.println("5b before 5a: order changed (unstable)");
    }
}
```

```text
Result: [1, 3, 5b, 5a]
5b before 5a: order changed (unstable)
```

### How is selection sort different from bubble sort?

| Feature | Selection Sort | Bubble Sort |
|---|---|---|
| Mechanism | Find minimum, one swap | Swap adjacent pairs |
| Swaps per pass | 1 | Many |
| Stable | No (basic) | Yes |
| Early exit | No | Yes (swapped flag) |
| Best on | Write-expensive scenarios | Nearly-sorted data |

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class SelectionVsBubble {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] a = {5, 3, 4, 1, 2};
        int selSwaps = selectionSortCount(a.clone());
        int bubSwaps = bubbleSortCount(a.clone());
        System.out.println("Selection swaps: " + selSwaps);
        System.out.println("Bubble swaps:    " + bubSwaps);
    }

    static int selectionSortCount(int[] arr) {
        int swaps = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length-1; i++) {
            int m = i;
            for (int j = i+1; j < arr.length; j++) if (arr[j] < arr[m]) m = j;
            if (m != i) { int t=arr[i]; arr[i]=arr[m]; arr[m]=t; swaps++; }
        }
        return swaps;
    }

    static int bubbleSortCount(int[] arr) {
        int swaps = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length-1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < arr.length-1-i; j++) {
                if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) { int t=arr[j]; arr[j]=arr[j+1]; arr[j+1]=t; swaps++; }
            }
        }
        return swaps;
    }
}
```

```text
Selection swaps: 3
Bubble swaps:    7
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Case | Time | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Best | O(n^2) | Always scans full unsorted suffix |
| Average | O(n^2) | Same |
| Worst | O(n^2) | Same |
| Space | O(1) | In-place |
| Swaps | O(n) | At most n - 1 |

### When should you use selection sort?

Only in two narrow cases: tiny arrays (a few elements) where the overhead does not matter, and situations where the number of writes must be absolutely minimised. In all other cases, use the library sort.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class WhenToUse {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Tiny array: selection sort is fine
        int[] tiny = {3, 1, 2};
        for (int i = 0; i < tiny.length-1; i++) {
            int m = i;
            for (int j = i+1; j < tiny.length; j++) if (tiny[j] < tiny[m]) m = j;
            int t = tiny[i]; tiny[i] = tiny[m]; tiny[m] = t;
        }
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(tiny));
    }
}
```

```text
[1, 2, 3]
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is assuming selection sort is stable. The long-distance swap breaks stability. Another mistake is not skipping the swap when `minIdx == i`, which is a wasted write.
